import json
from pathlib import Path
r=Path('content/base-reviewed.json');p=json.loads(r.read_text(encoding='utf8'));b={x['id']:x for x in p}
def note(id,text,**fields):b[id].update(nuance=text,keyTakeaway=text,**fields)
def q(id,prompt,options,reasons,translation):
 x=b[id]['quiz'][0];x.update(question=prompt,options=options,correctIndex=0,translation=translation,explanations=[dict(text=o,isCorrect=i==0,reason=reasons[i]) for i,o in enumerate(options)])
note('g2-l13-potential','The potential expresses ability or possibility. The object often takes が, but を also occurs in suitable constructions; do not treat it as universally wrong. Ru-verbs use られる in the standard form; colloquial ら抜き forms such as 食べれる are common but should be distinguished from the standard forms taught here. Some potential and passive/honorific forms overlap, so context matters.')
note('g2-l13-shi-reasons','し lists contributing reasons or characteristics and often implies that other reasons exist. A single explicit し clause is possible. Both plain and polite predicates can precede it; plain affirmative nouns/な-adjectives need だ. It is not the only way to combine several reasons, and から is not limited to just one reason.')
note('g2-l13-nara-conditional','なら takes a stated or assumed situation as a premise. It can narrow a topic (ひらがななら読める) or introduce advice based on the listener\'s plan. A narrowed topic suggests a contrast but does not logically prove that every other option is impossible. The action after なら may occur before the premise event, unlike a simple after-marker.')
note('g2-l15-volitional-to-omotte-iru','Volitional + と思う expresses intention. と思っている presents an ongoing intention, often held before speaking, but it need not be firm or guaranteed. A dictionary-form clause + と思う is also grammatical and reports a belief; it is a different construction from volitional intention.',connection='V volitional + と思う / と思っている')
note('g2-l16-to-ii-hoping','といい expresses a hoped-for situation. といいですね often shares a wish about the listener; といいんですが softens a personal wish, but the endings are not rigid person markers. For an action under the speaker\'s control, a potential form can make the desired possibility clearer: 行けるといい. Distinguish wishing from advising with ばいい/たらいい in other contexts.')
b['g2-l16-toki-tense'].update(nuance=b['gp-toki']['nuance'],keyTakeaway=b['gp-toki']['keyTakeaway'])
note('g2-l18-ba-yokatta','ばよかった often expresses regret about a choice that was not made. It does not by itself imply a specific dramatic result: 傘を持ってくればよかった says bringing an umbrella would have been better, not necessarily that the speaker is already drenched. Negative なければよかった expresses regret about doing something.')
note('g2-l21-te-aru','てある presents a resulting state or preparation from a deliberate action. In a state description the affected item often takes が; を can occur when emphasizing preparation already done. Contrast an intransitive ている state, which does not itself specify that someone deliberately prepared it.',connection='Transitive Vて + ある; affected item often が in a state description, or を in a preparation-focused construction')
note('g2-l22-noni-frustration','のに connects a fact with a result contrary to an expectation. Surprise, disappointment or criticism is common, but the contrast can also be favorable. It is not a universal marker of anger. Nouns and な-adjectives use なのに in the non-past affirmative. Distinguish this conjunction from purpose/use N/Vのに, as in 読むのに便利だ.')
note('g2-l23-temo-even-if','ても presents a concession: the conclusion still holds despite the condition. The condition can be hypothetical or an established fact. たら states a conditional or after-relation, so calling the two simple opposites hides their different uses. いくら…ても means no matter how much; だれが…ても means whoever.')
note('g2-l23-koto-ni-suru-naru','ことにする emphasizes a decision by an actor. ことになる presents a settled outcome or arrangement without emphasizing who made the decision; it does not prove that the speaker had no choice or that layoffs occurred. ことにしている presents a maintained policy, and ことになっている an established arrangement.')
q('g2-l13-potential','「食べることができません」という意味です。生魚が食べ（　　）。',['られません','させます','たいです','ました'],['食べられません is the standard potential negative.','食べさせます is causative: make/let eat.','食べたいです expresses desire.','食べました is past affirmative.'],'I cannot eat raw fish.')
q('g2-l13-shi-reasons','この部屋は（　　）し、駅にも近いです。',['静かだ','静か','静かな','静かで'],['A plain affirmative na-adjective takes だ before し.','The bare na-adjective stem does not attach directly to し.','な is the noun-modifying linker, not the ending before し.','で is connective but does not form the required predicate before し.'],'This room is quiet and is also near the station.')
q('g2-l15-volitional-to-omotte-iru','「登る」の意向形を使います。夏休みに富士山に（　　）と思っています。',['登ろう','登る','登り','登った'],['登ろう is the volitional and expresses intention with と思っている.','登ると思う can report a belief, but 登る is not the requested volitional form.','登り is the polite stem, not volitional.','登った is the plain past, not volitional.'],'Use the volitional form: I intend to climb Mount Fuji during summer vacation.')
b['g2-l14-tara-dou-desu-ka']['quiz'][0]['explanations'][3]['reason']='調べた plus the supplied たら would produce 調べたたら; the blank must contain 調べ.'
q('g2-l14-kamoshirenai','山田さんは道に迷っ（　　）かもしれません。',['た','て','たり','たのに'],['迷った is a past predicate before かもしれません.','The te-form does not directly connect to かもしれません.','たり needs a different continuation.','のに is a conjunction and does not form this possibility predicate.'],'Yamada may have got lost.')
for x in p:
 for quiz in x['quiz']:quiz['ruleHint']=x['connection']
r.write_text(json.dumps(p,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8')
